{
  "requirement": "During one call to `verify`, direct reads by the criterion and\nreads served by the SDK's agent tools record inputs in the same\n`ValidationContext`. For example, if criterion code reads a test and the agent\nthen reads its helper, both node fingerprints accompany the returned answer.\nA subsequent verification starts with an empty dependency list.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "Obligations from `requirements.sdk.graph.validation-context.shared_context`: (1) during one `verify`, direct reads by the criterion and reads served by agent tools record into the same `ValidationContext`. The example is that criterion code reads a node, the agent then reads another, and both fingerprints accompany the answer. (2) A subsequent verification starts with an empty dependency list.\n\nCoverage of (1):\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[verify_returns_direct_and_tool_reads_in_one_trace]` is the direct match to the example. Its `ReadThenAsk` criterion calls `cx.read` on node 42, then `cx.ask`. The mock agent backend calls `read_node` on node 0, which is node 7, then `submit_result`. The test asserts that `answer.dependencies()` is exactly `[Node(42), Node(7)]` with content fingerprints, so both direct and tool reads appear in order in one trace. It also asserts that the server saw 2 requests.\n- `configured_agent_uses_domain_source_tools_in_independent_executions` asserts the full dependency list. It combines the opening node, tool-read nodes and search-excerpt nodes, and it also checks that rejected calls and listings add nothing. This adds coverage of tool reads recorded in the verify result.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads]` shows that concurrent agent calls share one context's reads. It asserts that the dependencies are a union of both calls' reads and equal the finished result's dependencies.\n\nCoverage of (2): `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[a_second_verification_starts_with_an_empty_trace]` reuses one `Validator` for two verifications. The first returns only `[Node(1)]`. The second, on a different node, returns exactly `[Node(2, \"other\")]` and has no carry-over from the first. In `configured_agent_uses_domain_source_tools_in_independent_executions`, both loop iterations assert the identical full dependency list. That covers a repeat with tool reads, though each iteration makes a new validator.\n\nNo requirement-violating behavior is left undetected. A split context would fail the exact two-entry assertion, and leaked state would fail the second-verify equality assertion. No acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "39d480764fd0ae801a215f343637bb2966790fb61de3430afb83b7b29c13bd75",
